{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2131201979/10/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b1986d4b\" lines=\"19-37\">\n<description>\nRender the filled-square object counts as compact nested stacks ordered by count rank.\n\nInput structure\nA large noisy grid has a dominant background, scattered non-square noise, and\nfilled monochrome square objects. Each square side length has exactly one\nobject color, and side lengths form consecutive sizes.\n\nRule\nIgnore non-square components. If the lowest square in the scene is of the\nsmallest side length, treat that one smallest square as a distractor and do\nnot count it. For count rank k from left to right, create one block from all\nsquare sizes with at least k remaining objects; draw larger squares first and\nsmaller squares over their upper-left corner, then append one background\nseparator column.\n\nKey insight\nThe output is a count histogram of square side lengths, but each histogram\nslot is rendered as nested upper-left square masks using the side colors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"135a2760\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nRepair noisy periodic patterns inside frame-delimited grid regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Frame color (most common) forms full rows/cols, dividing the grid into rectangular regions.\n- bg (second most common) fills empty space; each region contains a small colored tile repeated periodically with a few corrupted pixels.\n\nRule:\n- For each region, find the bounding box of non-bg content.\n- Infer the smallest repeating tile that explains the pattern (majority vote per residue position), requiring it repeat at least 2x per axis.\n- Replace the region with the clean tiled version.\n\nKey insight: Tile dimensions are unknown and must be inferred; prefer the smallest tile with genuine repetition over one that memorizes the entire region.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9344f635\" lines=\"15-30\">\n<description>\nExtend each colored domino marker into a full row or column bar, with horizontal bars covering vertical bars at crossings.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Every foreground object is a 4-connected same-color domino of exactly two pixels.\n- A horizontal domino marks a row to paint; a vertical domino marks a column to paint.\n\nRule:\n- Paint each marked column entirely with the color of its vertical domino marker.\n- Paint each marked row entirely with the color of its horizontal domino marker.\n- Rows are painted after columns, so row bars overwrite column bars at intersections.\n\nKey insight:\n- The two-pixel orientation is the instruction: horizontal markers emit row bars, vertical markers emit column bars, and the only layering cue is row-over-column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e4888269\" lines=\"67-88\">\n<description>\nRecolor every target pixel by following the ordered two-column substitution legend.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background surrounds one multicolor vertical legend: a connected,\n  fully filled two-column block with one color pair per row.\n- Other non-background pixels or objects are the targets; divider or frame\n  pixels are ordinary target-side structure that remain unchanged unless their\n  color is rewritten by the legend.\n\nRule\n- Read the legend rows from top to bottom as substitutions from the left\n  column color to the right column color.\n- For each non-background pixel outside the legend, scan those rows in order;\n  whenever the current color equals the row's left color, replace it with the\n  row's right color and keep scanning lower rows.\n- Leave the legend itself and background pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The same color may be rewritten multiple times, but only by substitutions\n  that appear later in the vertical legend, so the row order is part of the\n  symbol instruction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e376de54\" lines=\"41-55\">\n<description>\nAlign parallel colored line segments so they all share the same start and end positions as a reference line.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several parallel line segments (diagonal, horizontal, or vertical), each a different color, running in the same direction but starting/ending at different positions.\n\nRule:\n- Detect the dominant direction shared by all segments.\n- Pick the median segment (by perpendicular offset) as the reference.\n- Shift every segment along the shared direction so its start aligns with the reference's start, then trim or extend it to match the reference's length.\n- Grid size stays the same; isolated single pixels are untouched.\n\nKey insight: The reference is the median line, not the longest; all segments are snapped to its extent regardless of their original length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9473c6fb\" lines=\"10-25\">\n<description>\nRecolor the sparse foreground pixels in scan order along their longer spread, cycling red, teal, and grey.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Every foreground object is a single isolated 4-connected pixel.\n- The foreground pixel set has a rectangular bbox whose row or column extent defines the scan direction.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the background unchanged.\n- If the foreground bbox is at least as tall as it is wide, order foreground pixels row-major; otherwise order them column-major.\n- Recolor the ordered pixels by repeating red, teal, grey from the first pixel onward.\n\nKey insight:\n- The original foreground colors are only markers; the output color depends on each marker's ordinal position in the dominant scan direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"cf5fd0ad\" lines=\"10-22\">\n<description>\nBuild a four-quadrant rotational tiling of the input square.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a square grid treated as one complete sprite.\n\nRule\n- Each output quadrant is a 2x2 repetition of a rotated copy of the whole input.\n- The quadrant rotations are upper-left 180 degrees, upper-right 90 degrees\n  clockwise, lower-left 90 degrees counterclockwise, and lower-right unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is a 4x4 grid of original-size blocks, not a per-pixel scale-up.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"902510d5\" lines=\"58-80\">\n<description>\nKeep the largest 8-connected motif and replace the corner/control pixels with a solid corner triangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Exactly one grid corner contains a foreground control pixel; it gives the\n  corner from which the triangle is painted.\n- There is one unique largest 8-connected monochrome foreground object, the\n  motif that remains in the output; all other foreground pixels are controls.\n- Every foreground component outside that motif is a singleton control pixel.\n- The fill color is the unique control color that appears in at least two\n  non-corner control pixels.\n\nRule\n- Start from a blank background canvas and redraw only the largest motif.\n- Count the non-corner control pixels; that count is the triangle side.\n- Paint the fill color on the right-triangle region where those two distances\n  from the marked corner's edges sum to less than the side length.\n\nKey insight\n- The scattered singleton controls are counted into a compact triangular\n  stack at the marked corner; only the largest motif is not part of the count.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a2d730bd\" lines=\"43-56\">\n<description>\nExtend each solid foreground rectangle toward its same-color isolated pixels with capped one-cell paths, clearing each original marker pixel.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each foreground color has exactly one solid rectangular object and one or more isolated same-color marker pixels.\n- Every marker is horizontally or vertically aligned with the rectangle bbox and lies outside it.\nRule\n- For each marker, draw a one-cell-wide straight path from the adjacent side of the rectangle through one cell beyond the marker.\n- Add a three-pixel perpendicular cap at the rectangle end and around the marker endpoint.\n- Recolor the original marker pixel to background.\nKey insight\n- The isolated pixels are not copied; they mark the hollow endpoints of capped protrusions grown from their matching rectangle.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"97d7923e\" lines=\"81-94\">\n<description>\nRecolor vertical bars by legend-driven rank selection.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Top-left: monochrome blocks touching row 0; each has color K and height k.\n- Main grid: vertical bars capped top and bottom by a single color, with solid-color interiors.\n\nRule:\n- For each legend entry (K, k), find vertical bars capped by K.\n- Rank bars by interior length (longest = rank 1).\n- Recolor the rank-k bar's interior to K.\n\nKey insight: Legend height is a rank index into length-sorted bars, not a literal size.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b2bc3ffd\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nMove every object above the solid bottom stripe upward by its own pixel count.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background, exactly one full-width non-background row\nthat acts as a fixed stripe, and one or more 4-connected monochrome objects\nabove that stripe.\n\nRule\nClear each object from its original cells, then translate it straight upward\nby the number of pixels in that object, preserving its shape and color. Leave\nthe full-width stripe unchanged.\n\nKey insight\nEach object's area is also its vertical displacement; the full-width row is a\nfixed baseline, not a moving object.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7e4d4f7c\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nCompress the L-shaped cue to three rows and add a pink copy of the top-row foreground mask.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color, a two-color top row, and all\nforeground pixels below the top row lie in the first column as vertical markers.\n\nRule\nKeep the input top row as output row 0 and the first marker row as output row 1.\nOutput row 2 is background except at the top-row foreground columns, which are\nrecolored pink.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
